{
	"nodes":[
		{"id":"620a09f904137728","type":"text","text":"## Trade Barriers\n\n### Tariffs\n\nTaxes (duties) levied on imported products and services. Allows governments to protect domestic suppliers and raise revenue.\n\nThe importer pays the tariff.\n\n### Quotas\n\nLimits placed on quantity of products that can be imported\n\n### Non-Tariff Barriers\n\nMeasures such as certification, licensing, sanctions or embargoes that make it more difficult and expensive for foreign producers to compete with domestic.\n\n### Embargoes/Sanctions\n\nMeasures that prohibit trade with a country\n\n\n\n","x":120,"y":-800,"width":760,"height":680},
		{"id":"ad1b1a82576bf478","type":"text","text":"## Imports and Exports\n\n* **Imports**: products and services produced outside a countries borders and brought into the country.\n\n* **Exports**: products and services produced in a countries borders and transported to another country or exported. \n\t* Japan exports consumer electronics.\n\n**International trade** provides consumers with **more choices** and **lower prices**, spurs competition.\n\n**Two major trends** have promoted international trade:\n\n* Fewer t**rade barriers**\n* Better **transportation** and **communication**\n\n","x":-640,"y":-120,"width":620,"height":400},
		{"id":"50fd4f871050a6eb","type":"text","text":"## Comparative Advantages Among Countries\n\n**Comparative advantage**: a countries ability to produce a good or service relatively more efficiently (at lower relative cost) than other countries.\n\nCountries should\n\n* **Export** goods in which they have **comparative advantage.**\n* **Import** goods in which they do **not have a comparative advantage**.\n\n","x":120,"y":280,"width":760,"height":480},
		{"id":"3f6a0fd5e134ca4b","type":"text","text":"# Balance of Payments\n\nTracks transactions between residents and non residents over a year. Includes:\n* individuals\n* companies\n* governments\n* government agencies","x":-1280,"y":-830,"width":800,"height":550},
		{"id":"38905b0c02301491","type":"text","text":"## Current Account (component of balance of payments)\n\nHow much money a country consumes and invests (outflows) vs how much it receives (inflows)\n\n* Goods and services account\n* Income account\n\t* A domestic business holds shares in a foreign corporation and receives dividends\n\t* Cross border employee wages paid to foreigners\n* Current Transfers account\n\t* Remittance (money sent home by workers)\n\t* Foreign aid (like GB to Ukraine for the war)\n\n![[Pasted image 20261022153527.png|321]]","x":-2120,"y":-1360,"width":760,"height":600},
		{"id":"a1a68837295a704b","type":"text","text":"# Capital and Financial Account (component of balance of payments)\n\n\nCapital account reports capital transfers between domestic entities and foreign entities such as debt forgiveness or transfer of assets by migrants in and out.\n\n![[Pasted image 20261022160556.png|447]]\n\n* **Direct investments** are long term investments between domestic and foreign entities, like Japan buying a production facility in the UK.\n* **Portfolio Investments** reflect the purchases and sales of securities such as equity and debt.\n* **Other investments** largely made up of loans and deposits between domestic entities and foreign entities.\n* **Reserve account** are transactions made by monetary authorities of a country like the central bank.\n\n\n","x":-2320,"y":-320,"width":840,"height":800},
		{"id":"aec5e07f8e714a71","type":"text","text":"## Current, Capital and Financial Account Relationship\n\n* Move in opposite direction\n* Sum of 'current account' and 'capital and financial account' should equal zero.","x":-1630,"y":720,"width":700,"height":480},
		{"id":"7bdd9c1293432a35","x":-2800,"y":-1360,"width":480,"height":450,"type":"text","text":"## Foreign Exchange Rate Systems\n\nInternational trade requires a system for exchanging currencies between nations because every country does not use the same type of money.\n\n**Exchange rate**: the rate at which one currency can be exchanged for another.\n\nInternational trade payments may be made in the countries domestic currency or in a foreign currency.\n\n### Three main types of exchange rate system\n\n* Fixed rate\n\t* does not allow for currency fluctuations - the value of a countries currency is tied to another currency or a commodity like gold.\n\t* eliminates currency risk (risk associated with fluctuation of exchange rates)\n\t* Causes issues if an economy becomes uncompetitive, current account worsens as currency becomes overvalued.\n\t* Only solution is for a country to devalue currency which central bank is reluctant to do.\n* Floating rate\n\t* driven by supply and demand (market) allowing the market to fix imbalances like current account deficits.\n* Managed floating rate\n\t* Same as floating rate but central bank intervenes to stabilise countries currency by buying domestic currency using foreign currency reserves.\n\n\n\n"},
		{"id":"9904ce10f82aa7b7","x":-2810,"y":-820,"width":570,"height":420,"type":"text","text":"## Value of Currencies \n\nMajor factors that affect a currencies value relative to other currencies (remember value goes up if demand for the currency goes up, driven by market):\n\n### Balance of payments\n\nCurrent account deficit leads to deprecation of currency value, exports become more competitive.\n\n### Inflation rate\n\nInflation leads to a depreciation of the domestic currency as a unit of the currency can buy less products.\n\n### Interest rates\n\nlead to appreciation of value of currency as investments into the country are more attractive.\n\n### Government debt\n\nHigh government debt leads to depreciation as risk government won't pay interest or principal.. Investors may sell their bonds and exchange the currency for another.\n\n### Political and economic environment\n\nInstability depreciates currency"},
		{"id":"4b358c567313d26b","x":-2720,"y":560,"width":640,"height":360,"type":"text","text":"## Foreign Exchange Market\n\nHighly integrated decentralised electronic network that connects buyers and sellers. Very active and liquid.\n\n* Bid exchange rate (bank or dealer will buy the currency)\n* Offer exchange rate (bank or dealer will sell the currency)\n\nDifference is the spread.\n\nSpot market: trades are immediate (spot rate)\nForward market: trades are agreed on an exchange rate and executed in the future (forward rate)."}
	],
